    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5747A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the environmental conservation law,\nin relation to commercial fishing licenses\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend and modify the eligibility\nlimitations for commercial fishing licenses and permits.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend Environmental Conservation Law\n(ECL) § 13-0328 to: 1) maintain a cap on the number of commercial food\nfish, crab, whelk (conch), and Marine and Coastal District Party and\nCharter Boat licenses or permits that may be issued; 2) maintain the\ncurrent moratorium on new lobster permits; and 3) maintain the current\neligibility requirements.\n\nSection 2 of this bill would make it effective December 31, 2017,\nprovided that any regulations necessary for the timely implementation\nof the provisions of this act on its effective date may be promulgated\non or before such date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSince ECL § 13-0328 was last amended in 2015, the number of commercial\nfishing licenses and permits issued has generally continued to decline\nas follows:\n\nÑ2015 Ñ2016*\nResident Food Fish Ñ932 Ñ907\nNon-Resident Food Fish Ñ41 Ñ41\nResident Crab Ñ541 Ñ527\nNon-Resident Crab Ñ25 Ñ25\nResident Lobster Ñ293 Ñ272\nNon-Resident Lobster Ñ18 Ñ18\nResident Whelk Ñ239 Ñ237\nNon-Resident Whelk Ñ10 Ñ11\nParty & Charter Boat Ñ488 Ñ492\n*Issued as of 11-1-2016\n\nHowever,  the  current  numbers  of licenses and permits are still too\nhigh to ensure economic viability of commercial fisheries,  given  the\nrestrictions and limits on fishing activity required under current and\nanticipated  federal  and  interstate  fishery management regulations.\nAccordingly, this bill would provide a  mechanism  for  their  further\nreduction  over  the  next four years. The maximum number of party and\ncharter boat licenses that may  be  issued  to  uninspected  passenger\nvessels would remain at 517.\n\nFurther,  a  complete  moratorium  would  be  maintained on commercial\nlobster permits, given that  significant  excess  harvesting  capacity\nexists   in  the  lobster  fishery,  and  current  lobster  management\nregulations do not provide for issuance of the required  lobster  trap\ntags  to any new persons. The substantial decline in lobster abundance\nthat has resulted from episodes of mortality beginning  in  1999  also\nsupports continuing to prevent new entrants in this troubled fishery.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nECL  § 13-0328 was last amended to extend and modify its provisions in\nChapter 212 of the Laws of 2015.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis  act  shall  take  effect on December 31, 2017, provided that any\nregulations necessary for the timely implementation of the  provisions\nof this act on its effective date may be promulgated on or before such\ndate.\n",
